{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794827714,"nonce":"Y7u0kqV4oFb52kAkA4OsLEUqIlY=","message":"Ninth nAIR slice, 27612f9d: the compiler's byte intrinsics and throw. The refusal reason now names a call's callee kind and an intrinsic's name, and it showed what the std is made of: 97 of the 113 refused calls were TIdent intrinsics ($bytes_sizebits, $bget, $bset, $new, $aalloc, $ttype, $ref ...), the compiler's own operations, not calls. $bytes_sizebits, $bget and $bset are lowered for 32-bit integer and 64-bit float buffers (the element type is the type argument of the hl.BytesAccess the buffer is typed as; the abstract's name is qualified, hl.BytesAccess). throw ends the block with the value as a Dynamic; an object converts to Dynamic as an unsafe cast, since the runtime boxes values and never objects. A test lowers the std's own hl.types.ArrayBytes_Int.reverse from the TAST and runs it on a runtime-shaped array, and a throwing function of the program on both sides of its condition. Std bodies that lower on their own: 87 to 107 of 400; ArrayBytes_Int 3 to 12 of 35 (push, concat, splice, insert, remove, copy and resize among them), though push still needs __expand, which needs hl.Bytes allocation (a native), and contains needs Null\u003cInt\u003e unboxing. Remaining std frontier by count: generic classes (ArrayObj, ArrayBytes), OBJECT_DECL, UI16 and F32 buffers, $new, Null\u003cT\u003e and Dyn conversions, calls through a LOCAL or a dynamic field.","files":null}]}